  "result": "`summarize()` now handles 200,000 orders in well under a second, and its output is unchanged. `npm test` passes all 4 tests, including the new ones.\n\nThe slowness came from three things in `src/report.js`, and I fixed each:\n- **Duplicate check:** an array `includes` scan became a `Set`.\n- **Customer lookup:** an array `find` became a `Map`.\n- **Sorting:** the full leaderboard was re-sorted after every order. It is now sorted once at the end. This gives the same ordering because `byRank` is a total order, since `customerId` is unique per group.\n\nI added two tests to `test/report.test.js`:\n- **Behaviour check:** the test embeds a copy of the original slow implementation and compares exact output (rank, field order and ordering) on randomized data. The data covers ties, duplicate ids, refunds, pending and EUR orders, and several limits. It would catch any behaviour change.\n- **Speed check:** a 200,000-order run must finish in under 1 second.\n\nThe speed test asserts a wall-clock limit, so it could occasionally fail on a heavily loaded machine. I didn't measure the actual run time on its own.",
